WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.200
Taylor Swift has dropped the music video

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:04.120
for her latest single, "The Man."

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:05.758
Both the song and video take on

00:00:05.758 --> 00:00:08.060
double standards between men and women.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:09.887
As with her past videos, this one is

00:00:09.887 --> 00:00:12.280
full of Easter eggs and
other hidden meanings.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:14.290
Here's a look at everything you missed.

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:15.940
Every video from Swift's "Lover" era

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:17.620
has started with her by a window,

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:19.230
and "The Man" is no different.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.780
Except now we have Tyler Swift.

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:23.490
The office he's in
looks similar to the one

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:25.433
from Swift's "Bad Blood" music video.

00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:27.370
Instead of a fight scene between women,

00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:28.811
this time we get a recreation

00:00:28.811 --> 00:00:30.152
of "The Wolf of Wall Street."

00:00:30.152 --> 00:00:32.310
Swift's alpha-male persona, Tyler,

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.890
mimics Leonardo DiCaprio's Jordan Belfort.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:36.400
Tyler takes the scene a step further

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:37.925
by fist-bumping and acknowledging

00:00:37.925 --> 00:00:39.520
only the men in the room.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.469
The Leo reference is
no coincidence either,

00:00:41.469 --> 00:00:43.150
but more on him later.

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:46.003
Next, we see Tyler
manspreading on a subway train.

00:00:46.003 --> 00:00:47.840
Behind him are some fake advertisements

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.390
parodying the overtly
violent and sexual ways

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:52.137
in which the media portrays men.

00:00:52.137 --> 00:00:54.172
The one seemingly advertising for liquor

00:00:54.172 --> 00:00:56.420
reads, "Capitalize on the feeling."

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:57.786
This is likely a reference to how

00:00:57.786 --> 00:01:00.095
Swift's been criticized
and called "calculated"

00:01:00.095 --> 00:01:02.020
for writing about her relationships.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.160
The word greedy is
written on the train wall.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:05.740
Swift's been called greedy in the past

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:07.810
for certain business decisions she's made,

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:10.190
like adopting a fan-verified
ticket-sales system

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:12.120
for her "Reputation" stadium tour

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.430
and pulling her music catalog
from streaming services

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:15.970
like Spotify and Apple Music

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:17.700
over artist royalty disputes.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:19.580
But there's a whole other take we can read

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.760
from this poster and the word greedy.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:24.190
The title "Bo$$ Scotch"
seems to be a play on words

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:27.180
referring to her old record-label
boss Scott Borchetta.

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:28.970
In a post on Tumblr last June,

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:30.470
Swift shared how she felt betrayed

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:31.870
by Borchetta's sale of her music

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:33.700
to talent manager Scooter Braun.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:35.542
This poster could be
a callout to Borchetta

00:01:35.542 --> 00:01:38.410
as greedy for capitalizing
on Swift's body of work,

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:41.550
which famously features her
own feelings and experiences.

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:43.557
Let's take a look back
at the movie poster:

00:01:43.557 --> 00:01:45.630
"Man vs Disaster."

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:47.730
The fake title could reference
the action-film trope

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:50.360
that all it takes is
one man to save the day.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.225
In contrast, Swift has often been said

00:01:52.225 --> 00:01:53.740
to be playing the victim

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:55.440
when she tries to fight industry giants

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.870
like record labels and streaming services.

00:01:57.870 --> 00:01:59.730
One of the fellow train
passengers is wearing

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:02.260
a "Miss Americana &amp; the
Heartbreak Prince" sweatshirt

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:04.747
straight from Swift's actual merch line.

00:02:04.747 --> 00:02:07.120
"Miss Americana" is
another song from "Lover"

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:09.680
that deals with themes
of sexism and misogyny.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:11.460
Looking back to the movie poster,

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.340
you'll see the date July 20.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:14.850
Does this mean "Miss Americana" could be

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:17.050
a single in the summer of 2020?

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:20.217
Tyler opens a newspaper,
and the cover story asks,

00:02:20.217 --> 00:02:22.560
"What man won the year
in celebrity dating?"

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:24.330
This headline calls
out the double standard

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:26.654
that criticizes women but celebrates men

00:02:26.654 --> 00:02:28.062
for dating around.

00:02:28.062 --> 00:02:30.340
Swift herself is somewhat
of a poster child

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:31.620
for this double standard

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:34.200
and has spoken out against
it many times in the past.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.600
Tyler exits the train onto a platform

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.020
and pauses to, uh, take a leak.

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:40.790
He's at 13th Street Station,

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:42.950
a nod to Swift's lucky number, 13,

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:44.900
which we've seen referenced
many times before

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:46.360
in past videos.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:48.590
The wall Tyler stops at
is covered in graffiti

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:50.830
with the names of Swift's past albums,

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:54.200
like "Reputation," "Red," and "1989."

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.220
She's hidden Easter eggs
through graffiti before,

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:58.380
in many of her "Reputation"-era videos,

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:00.430
like "Ready for It?" and "Delicate."

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:02.540
We also see the word karma in graffiti,

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:03.900
which we've heard Swift use before

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.371
in her clapback song "Look
What You Made Me Do."

00:03:06.371 --> 00:03:09.220
♪ Not for me, all I think about is karma ♪

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:10.467
A sign says, "Missing:

00:03:10.467 --> 00:03:12.580
If found, return to Taylor Swift."

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:15.140
And another one symbolizes no scooters.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:17.160
The posters and graffiti
are another reference

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:18.935
to her ongoing battle with Scooter Braun

00:03:18.935 --> 00:03:20.976
and Scott Borchetta over the masters

00:03:20.976 --> 00:03:23.550
of all of the albums we see on the wall.

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:24.742
Tyler's peeing on the wall

00:03:24.742 --> 00:03:26.183
could represent how the two men,

00:03:26.183 --> 00:03:28.250
and men in the music industry in general,

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:30.760
have disrespected Swift
throughout her career.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:33.460
There's also a poster for "Mr. Americana,"

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:34.980
directed by Larry Wilson,

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.320
premiering at "Mandance,"

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:38.500
and documenting Tyler Swift.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:40.314
Taylor Swift's own documentary,

00:03:40.314 --> 00:03:43.070
"Miss Americana," directed by Lana Wilson,

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:45.220
premiered at Sundance earlier this year.

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:47.420
When speaking to Glamour
about making the film,

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:49.700
Wilson recalled how she
and her all-female crew

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:52.440
were often not taken seriously
because of their gender.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.140
This riff on the documentary's poster

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:55.650
also relates to the fact that Swift

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:57.730
directed this music video for "The Man."

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:00.120
Her impressive credits
for the song and video,

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:03.560
that's songwriter, performer,
executive producer,

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:06.140
video director, and star, to be clear,

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:07.840
come at a time when music, film,

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:09.270
and other entertainment industries

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:10.860
are being heavily criticized for

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:14.020
their lack of opportunities
and recognition for women.

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:16.120
As Tyler steps away from the subway wall,

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:18.185
we see "The Man" written out in...

00:04:18.185 --> 00:04:20.220
well, it's glittery and blue,

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:22.950
which looks pretty similar
to Swift's "Lover" aesthetic.

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:24.870
Having "The Man" written in blue glitter

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:26.440
could also be Swift's way of saying

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:29.140
men, just like women, can be complex

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:30.845
and aren't limited to
the narrow definition

00:04:30.845 --> 00:04:33.310
of masculinity society pushes on them.

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:35.330
Next, Tyler's on a yacht.

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:36.945
If it looks familiar, that's because it's

00:04:36.945 --> 00:04:39.260
another nod to "The Wolf of Wall Street."

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:41.950
The movie not only embodies
the sexist double standards

00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:43.460
Swift calls out in "The Man,"

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:45.360
but its star, Leonardo DiCaprio,

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:47.993
is specifically
name-checked in the lyrics:

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:52.030
DiCaprio is well known for dating

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:54.810
a number of women,
especially younger models.

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:56.453
And in "The Man," Swift makes a point

00:04:56.453 --> 00:04:58.360
of emphasizing how men like DiCaprio

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:01.010
get more of a "legend" status
for this type of dating,

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:02.730
whereas women like herself are criticized

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:04.010
for their dating habits.

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:06.150
We see Swift call out
double standards again

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:09.030
as Tyler leaves what looks
like a one-night stand.

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:10.610
Instead of having the walk of shame

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:11.820
that's assigned to women,

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:13.236
he struts through what looks like

00:05:13.236 --> 00:05:15.311
more of a hall of fame.

00:05:15.311 --> 00:05:17.090
19 hands high-five him.

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:18.490
They're the 19 hands we saw

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:20.560
in the song's promotional photo.

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:22.400
19 is a number Swift's been highlighting

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:25.000
since even before the video was announced.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.030
During her woman of the
decade acceptance speech

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:28.710
at the Billboard Music Awards,

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:31.730
Swift mentioned 19 other
female artists by name.

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:33.375
The references to the number are likely

00:05:33.375 --> 00:05:35.059
a nod to the 19th Amendment,

00:05:35.059 --> 00:05:36.475
which gave the first group of women

00:05:36.475 --> 00:05:38.110
the right to vote in the US.

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:40.620
The amendment's ratification
is seen as a landmark win

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:43.060
in the women's rights
movement in American history.

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:45.730
Swift also released "The
Man" on February 27.

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:47.840
This is the anniversary
of when the Supreme Court

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:49.620
dismissed a challenge to the amendment

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:51.750
and unanimously agreed
that the right to vote

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:54.260
could not be denied on the basis of sex.

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:55.840
And, if the hallway looks familiar,

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:57.580
it could be because it resembles the one

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:00.980
from Kanye West's distinctly sparse home.

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:03.310
You might recall that West
infamously interrupted

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:07.400
a 19-year-old Taylor
Swift at the 2009 VMAs.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:09.560
And Swift's not just
throwing shade at West here.

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:11.240
Her reference is pretty significant

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:13.270
under the context of "The Man."

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:15.250
When West interrupted her award speech,

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:18.210
he implied that her work
wasn't deserving enough of it.

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:19.980
Years later, when West referenced Swift

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:21.018
in his song "Famous,"

00:06:21.018 --> 00:06:23.620
he rapped, "I made that b---- famous."

00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:25.320
West's behavior is a clear example

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:27.990
of men devaluing or even
straight up taking credit

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:29.510
for a woman's work.

00:06:29.510 --> 00:06:31.360
This reference seems even more purposeful

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:33.280
after a scene in "Miss Americana"

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:36.050
that shows Swift writing
"The Man" with Joel Little.

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:38.492
She throws out some lyric ideas, saying:

00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:45.770
Which is exactly what West did.

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:47.900
In the next scene,
Tyler's being celebrated

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:49.690
as the world's greatest dad

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:52.560
after seemingly doing the
bare minimum with his child.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:54.480
This scene addresses
another double standard,

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:56.990
where single moms rarely
get any recognition,

00:06:56.990 --> 00:06:58.280
whereas their male counterparts

00:06:58.280 --> 00:06:59.780
are praised for little effort.

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:01.408
If you look closely, you can also see

00:07:01.408 --> 00:07:03.450
that while Tyler is manspreading again,

00:07:03.450 --> 00:07:05.260
his daughter isn't, which could be showing

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:06.840
how girls are taught from a young age

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:08.275
to take up less space.

00:07:08.275 --> 00:07:09.480
Also, catch a brief cameo

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:11.500
from social-media star Dominic Toliver

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:13.260
and actress Jayden Bartels.

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:15.730
We next see Tyler partying
in a bar with other men.

00:07:15.730 --> 00:07:16.620
They throw around money

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:18.760
as they take body shots off of women,

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:21.010
referencing the reduction
of women to objects

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:23.090
and being valued only for their bodies.

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:25.240
The money they throw around
has Tyler's face on it,

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:27.620
a pretty obvious comment
to men owning more money

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:29.050
because of the gender pay gaps

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:31.410
and a straight-up lack of
opportunities for women.

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:33.420
The men are all wearing
matching gold rings,

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:36.000
which look similar to
fraternity or other club rings.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:37.970
This could be referencing
the boys'-club culture

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:39.120
in many industries,

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:40.840
especially the music industry.

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:43.180
Next up, Tyler's playing
a match of tennis.

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:45.417
We can spot a Taylor
Swift merch water bottle

00:07:45.417 --> 00:07:46.570
under the bench.

00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:47.750
This scene seems to reference

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:49.930
a specific incident with Serena Williams,

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:53.160
when she was fined $17,000
for code violations

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:55.140
at the 2018 US Open.

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:56.550
Williams argued with the umpire

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:58.790
and slammed her racket on
the ground during a match.

00:07:58.790 --> 00:08:00.298
She said her actions were due to her

00:08:00.298 --> 00:08:01.598
feeling that the umpire was

00:08:01.598 --> 00:08:03.820
unfairly penalizing her with his calls.

00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:05.783
Serena's situation relates to Swift's

00:08:05.783 --> 00:08:07.740
recent comment to CBS "Sunday Morning,"

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:09.517
when she said, a man can react;

00:08:09.517 --> 00:08:11.330
"a woman can only overreact."

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:13.870
In the music video, Tyler's
given a similar pass.

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:16.080
He throws a tantrum, gets hysterical,

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:18.600
smashes his racket, and
fights with the umpire.

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:20.430
And no one does a thing.

00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:21.420
Not the ball girl,

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:23.480
who's actually TikTok star Loren Gray,

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:24.550
or even the umpire,

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:26.440
played by Swift's own father, Scott.

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:28.450
This could symbolize how people do notice

00:08:28.450 --> 00:08:30.470
men acting childish and emotional,

00:08:30.470 --> 00:08:32.770
but they don't face the
same backlash that women do,

00:08:32.770 --> 00:08:35.020
and often for even lesser behavior.

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:38.610
58 years later, a very aged
Tyler is getting married

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:40.480
to a much younger woman.

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:44.470
Five plus eight is, of course, 13.

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:46.360
The scene shows a stereotypical marriage

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:49.010
between a super-old
guy and a younger girl.

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:51.060
As the bride flashes her giant ring,

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:52.950
it's likely she'll be painted the bad guy

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:55.000
and labeled a gold digger.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:56.860
The light pink flower on Tyler's lapel

00:08:56.860 --> 00:08:58.330
looks like the cornelia flowers

00:08:58.330 --> 00:09:00.970
Swift's been using throughout
her "Lover"-era promo

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:04.150
and also references the album's
track "Cornelia Street."

00:09:04.150 --> 00:09:05.950
We've seen a wedding and cake smashing

00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:08.100
in a "Lover"-era video before;

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:09.460
in "You Need to Calm Down,"

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:10.726
there's an epic cake fight

00:09:10.726 --> 00:09:12.370
after a same-sex marriage.

00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:14.286
Swift could be drawing parallels

00:09:14.286 --> 00:09:16.535
to society's acceptance
of what might be called

00:09:16.535 --> 00:09:19.067
a nontraditional marriage
with a larger age gap

00:09:19.067 --> 00:09:21.400
over one between people of the same sex.

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:24.180
The scene also begs us
to imagine the reverse.

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:26.610
If an older woman were
marrying a younger man,

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:29.310
she'd be the one criticized
for "cradle robbing."

00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:30.968
In any case, it's the straight man

00:09:30.968 --> 00:09:32.443
who's getting off easy.

00:09:32.443 --> 00:09:34.730
As the epic video comes to an end,

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:37.920
Swift reverses gender roles one last time.

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:39.613
We hear Swift, as the video's director,

00:09:39.613 --> 00:09:43.395
yell, "Cut!" and Tyler walks off set.

00:09:43.395 --> 00:09:45.110
He asks Swift about his performance,

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:47.580
but the voice we hear
is actually The Rock's.

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:51.890
Swift tells him to be
"sexier, maybe more likable."

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:54.440
She then praises Loren
for her outstanding work,

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:56.100
when really she's just
been standing around

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:57.550
in the background.

00:09:57.550 --> 00:09:59.441
It's Swift's last dig at the ridiculous

00:09:59.441 --> 00:10:02.000
and sexist standards in
the entertainment industry,

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:04.400
where men are showered
with praise and awards

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:06.340
often for doing the bare minimum,

00:10:06.340 --> 00:10:09.530
while women are constantly
asked to do better by...

00:10:09.530 --> 00:10:13.167
well, by being sexier and more likable.

00:10:13.167 --> 00:10:15.150
"The Man" is a funny but poignant take

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:17.290
on several of the double
standards women face

00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:19.420
and the larger systems that enforce them.

00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:21.310
Swift's "Lover" era has seen her becoming

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:22.950
more vocal about politics,

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:25.170
and, if this video is any indication,

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:27.003
she's only getting started.

